title: How plugins work
section: Plugins
desc: What a bddr plugin is, how to add one to your project, and which plugins ship with bddr.
lede: A plugin bundles fixtures, hooks, steps and parameter types. bddr core depends on no browser or HTTP library. The plugin and the library it wraps are dependencies of your project.
---
<h2>The plugins that ship with bddr</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Plugin</th><th>Wraps</th><th>Fixtures</th><th>Page</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>@bddr/playwright</code></td><td>Playwright</td><td><code>browser</code>, <code>context</code>, <code>page</code>, <code>request</code>, <code>expect</code>, <code>newContext</code>, <code>playwright</code></td><td><a href="playwright.html">Playwright</a></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>@bddr/selenium</code></td><td>selenium-webdriver</td><td><code>driver</code>, <code>open</code>, <code>wait</code>, <code>newDriver</code>, <code>selenium</code></td><td><a href="selenium.html">Selenium</a></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>@bddr/axios</code></td><td>axios</td><td><code>api</code>, <code>newApi</code>, <code>axios</code></td><td><a href="axios.html">axios</a></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>They live in <code>plugins/</code> in the repository. Each is a separate package, not part of the bddr workspace, so the bddr packages never depend on Playwright or Selenium.</p>

<h2>Add one to your project</h2>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><h4>Install the plugin and the library</h4>
    <p>Until bddr is published, install the plugin from your clone. The command for each plugin is on its page and in <a href="getting-started.html">Getting started</a>.</p>
    <figure class="code-block"><pre><code class="lang-bash">$ npm i -D file:../bddr/plugins/axios axios</code></pre></figure></li>
  <li><h4>List it in the config</h4>
<cb title="bddr.config.ts">import { defineConfig } from '@bddr/cli';
import { axios } from '@bddr/axios';

export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [axios({ baseURL: 'https://api.example.com' })],
});</cb>
    <p>Without TypeScript types you can write the plugin by name: <code>plugins: [['@bddr/axios', { baseURL: '...' }]]</code>.</p></li>
  <li><h4>Import it once for types</h4>
<cb title="steps/support/types.ts">import '@bddr/axios';</cb>
    <p>This types <code>ctx.api</code>, <code>({ api })</code> and <code>inject('api')</code> in your step files.</p></li>
  <li><h4>Use the fixture</h4>
<cb title="steps/products.steps.ts">When('I list the products', async ({ api, state }) => {
  state.set('res', await api.get('/products'));
});</cb></li>
</ol>

<h2>Several plugins together</h2>
<p>List as many as you need. Their fixture names do not clash, so one scenario can drive a browser and call an API. See <a href="mixed.html">UI and API in one scenario</a>.</p>
<cb>plugins: [
  playwright({ browser: 'chromium' }),
  axios({ baseURL: 'https://api.example.com' }),
],</cb>

<h2>Options from the command line</h2>
<p>Plugins read run-time overrides from <code>-D</code>, such as <code>-D browser=firefox</code>, <code>-D headed=true</code> or <code>-D baseURL=https://staging.example.com</code>. Each plugin page lists them.</p>

<h2>Plugin facts</h2>
<ul>
  <li>A plugin's <code>setup</code> runs once per process, in the main process and in every worker process, before your step files load.</li>
  <li>A name is looked up from the config folder. Paths that start with <code>.</code> are relative to the config file.</li>
  <li>A missing plugin is a usage error and the run exits with code 2.</li>
  <li>A scenario that uses none of a plugin's fixtures starts nothing from it. An API-only scenario never opens a browser.</li>
</ul>
